/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> feetHihatFootLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "The left foot is the drummer's fourth limb, and in most beginners it does nothing. A crisp hi-hat \"chick\" on 2 and 4 — or on every quarter — gives the groove a second pulse, frees the right hand for the ride and is the base of jazz, Latin and fusion playing.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Heel down and rock the toes onto the hi-hat pedal so the cymbals close with a short, crisp \"chick\", lift them just enough to open again — the ride, snare and kick keep going exactly as before.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Line 1 is the reference: chick on 2 and 4 with the snare. Line 2 chicks every quarter, line 3 every \"and\" — the hardest, because foot and hand alternate. Lines 4, 5 and 8 move the kick while the left foot stays put; line 6 moves the right hand to the bell; line 7 drops the cymbal entirely so the pedal is the only time-keeper.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "Jazz drummers from Philly Joe Jones to Jeff Hamilton (feathered kick, hi-hat on 2 and 4), Steve Gadd and Dave Weckl in fusion, and rock ballads where the ride takes over and the foot keeps the hat alive.",
  ),
];
